# TEMPLATE: wild bet. Unconventional tactics, stunts, giveaways, personal outreach.
#
# THE 2 TESTS. This only belongs in WLD if it passes BOTH:
#
#   1. It is NOT a repeatable channel with its own economics.
#      If you can run it weekly and compute a CAC, it belongs in its real module.
#   2. Its payoff is ASYMMETRIC. Bounded cost, unbounded or unmeasurable upside,
#      usually invisible to standard attribution.
#
# Where the line falls:
#   giveaway that is really a discount              -> 03-OFF
#   giveaway engineered to produce 400 UGC posts    -> 19-WLD
#   handwritten note in every order, costed         -> 17-CXP
#   personal letter to your 50 highest-LTV buyers   -> 19-WLD
#   referral program with tracked codes and payback -> 03-OFF
#   referral stunt built on a one-off moment        -> 19-WLD
#
# The test is repeatability and measurability, not how creative it feels.
#
# cost_ceiling: REQUIRED. The bounded downside, in money and in time.
#   A wild bet with an unbounded downside is not a wild bet, it is a gamble.
#   e.g. cost_ceiling: 40k INR and 2 days of founder time, 50 letters
#
# tier: expect 4. These sources are threads, podcast anecdotes and founder stories
#   with no numbers. Tier them honestly and let retrieval deprioritise them.
#   That is correct. A tier 4 wild bet that gets tested once and works becomes your
#   own tier 0 evidence, and nobody else will have it.
#
# In the `why` line, capture 4 things if the source gives them:
#   - the mechanism: why it MIGHT work, as a hypothesis not a finding
#   - the cost ceiling
#   - what would make it repeatable if it works
#   - how it backfires. Reputational risk is real here and most sources ignore it.
#
# Do NOT invent an expected effect. Wild bets do not have reliable expected effects
# and pretending otherwise poisons the rest of the library.
